/* Legacy theme for the generic confirm dialog. CCommonMessageBox's own native sprite composite
   (back-fill + top cap + middle fill + bottom cap) moved to generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rcss -- see
   that file's own comment for why (item3D layering: RmlUi's main context, which this document is
   part of, always composites LAST in the frame, so the item would otherwise always be painted
   over by the panel's own frame art). This file is layout/content only now, except for
   `.gcd-header-rail`'s own sprite decorator below (an ordinary opaque foreground image, not a
   compositing-order concern the way item3D is -- see that rule's own comment).

   #panel is centered via the .center-both utility class (generic_confirm_dialog.rml); every
   dimension is dp -- this is a LayoutMode::Dialog window (no transform:scale(root_scale)), same
   category as remember_password_prompt.rcss/msg_win.rcss, not the root_scale-tracking inventory-
   family windows -- so `dp`, not `-px`, is correct here (see base.rcss's header-rail-family
   comment for why that distinction matters). 230x160 matches native's own MSGBOX_WIDTH=230 and
   base 2-line height (67+50=117) plus room to grow -- see the fixed-panel simplification note in
   docs/rmlui-ui-system/dialog-migration-plan.md. No background-color/border here anymore -- the
   sprite composite now lives in generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rcss's own same-sized #panel, painted
   before this document composites. */
#panel {
	width: 230dp;
	height: 160dp;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 12dp;
	color: white;
}

/* Native's own IMAGE_MSGBOX_TOP_TITLEBAR sprite (newui_Message_03.tga, CMessageBoxMng /
   MessageBox.cpp) -- a title-banner variant of the plain top-cap art (newui_msgbox_top.tga,
   already ported as .gcd-bg-sprite-top in generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rcss), used natively by the
   CustomMessageBox.cpp `*MenuMsgBox` family as a drop-in swap for the plain top cap whenever the
   box has a title, with title text drawn directly on top. Same 230x67 slot/size as the plain
   version (confirmed via each source image's own header) so it overlays that exact screen rect
   with no seam. Lives in the FOREGROUND document (this file), not generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rcss
   -- unlike item3D, a title banner has no compositing-order reason to sit behind anything; an
   ordinary opaque foreground overlay is simplest and avoids adding a second data-model to the bg
   document (deliberately kept 100% static -- see this dialog's scrollbar-fix history for why
   dynamic bg-document state was rejected there too). */
@spritesheet msgbox-titlebar-sheet
{
	src: "../../../newui_Message_03.tga";
	msgbox-titlebar-image: 0px 0px 230px 67px;
}

/* A direct #panel child, sibling of .gcd-body, not nested inside it -- positioned in #panel's own
   coordinate space (edge-to-edge, matching .gcd-bg-sprite-top's own box exactly) rather than
   flowing with .gcd-body's own 25dp-inset content area, since the sprite is authored for the
   panel's full 230dp width. No severity color variants of the art -- native has no concept of a
   colored title banner, and this dialog's severity field has zero real consumers today (see
   dialog-migration-plan.md); severity stays a text-color-only distinction (below). */
.gcd-header-rail {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	right: 0;
	top: 0;
	height: 67dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	decorator: image(msgbox-titlebar-image);
}
/* Title text drawn directly over the sprite above, matching native's own
   (GetPos().x+10, GetPos().y+10) inset (CustomMessageBox.cpp's *MenuMsgBox::RenderTexts() family,
   the sprite's own native consumers) -- simplified to one centered line rather than replicating
   exact pixel-wrap math, same simplification this whole port already uses elsewhere. No conflict
   with .gcd-body's own top:35dp below -- native's title sits well above that, at y:10. */
.gcd-header-title {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	right: 0;
	top: 10dp;
	text-align: center;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-weight: bold;
	color: #ffd23c;
	pointer-events: none;
}
.gcd-header-title.warning { color: #ffb040; }
.gcd-header-title.error { color: #ff4040; }

/* Text floats transparently over the sprite composite below it (painted by the background
   document), matching native (no separate boxed "well" -- CCommonMessageBox draws its text
   directly over its own composited background). left/right/top match native's own text metrics
   (MSGBOX_TEXT_TOP_BLANK=35,
   MSGBOX_TEXT_MAXWIDTH=180 out of the 230-wide panel = ~25dp side margin); bottom leaves a small
   gap above the buttons (repositioned below).

   top+bottom (not top+height) deliberately -- this engine's box model is content-box only (no
   box-sizing support anywhere in this codebase), so an explicit height combined with a bordered/
   padded box can push past the intended bottom edge (this bit us on the first pass of this file).
   Anchoring both edges keeps the box's outer size exactly (right - left) x (bottom - top)
   regardless of its own content.

   display:flex row: #gcd_item3d_anchor (native C3DItemCommonMsgBox's icon, left) + .gcd-text-col
   (fills the rest, right) -- see the .rml's own comment for why row not column. The optional
   title banner is NOT a child of this box (see .gcd-header-rail/.gcd-header-title above) -- it
   overlays #panel's own top edge instead, independent of this box's own fixed top:35dp. */
.gcd-body {
	position: absolute;
	left: 25dp;
	right: 25dp;
	top: 35dp;
	bottom: 56dp;
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: row;
	justify-content: center;
	align-items: center;
}
/* Holds lines/input-row/progress-track -- everything .gcd-body used to flex-center directly
   before #gcd_item3d_anchor needed to sit beside it instead of above/below it (see that element's
   own comment below and the .rml's). Fills .gcd-body's full height explicitly (rather than
   relying on align-items, which wouldn't stretch under align-items:center) so its own
   overflow:auto has a real bound to scroll within once content (mainly `lines`) grows taller than
   the panel -- see the scrollbar rules below.

   overflow is `overflow-x, overflow-y` (CSS shorthand order) -- `hidden auto`, NOT `auto hidden`,
   is deliberate: horizontal never needs to scroll (`.gcd-line` wraps within a fixed width), and
   this engine only ever instantiates a scrollbarvertical element when overflow-y is Auto/Scroll,
   never Hidden (confirmed against RmlUi/Source/Core/Layout/ContainerBox.cpp) -- getting this
   order backwards silently means no scrollbar is ever created, regardless of text length.

   pointer-events: auto is required here too -- base.rcss's `body { pointer-events: none; }`
   inherits down to every descendant that doesn't explicitly opt back in (same reason `.btn`/
   `.checkbox-row` each set their own `pointer-events: auto`), and the scrollbar's generated
   slidertrack/sliderbar are real DOM children of THIS element, so they inherit whatever this
   element's own computed value is -- without this, the whole scrollbar (track clicks, thumb drag)
   silently can't receive any mouse events at all. */
.gcd-text-col {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	justify-content: center;
	align-items: center;
	height: 100%;
	flex: 1 1 auto;
	overflow: hidden auto;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
/* Minimal flat-color scrollbar (no decorator images, matching this file's own progress-bar/keypad
   styling) -- .gcd-text-col is the first scrollable element in this theme, so there's no existing
   scrollbar styling to reuse; RmlUi generates real child elements for these (scrollbarvertical/
   slidertrack/sliderbar), not pseudo-elements. Arrow buttons collapsed to 0 size -- drag-only,
   simplest usable affordance for a narrow well. */
.gcd-text-col scrollbarvertical {
	width: 6dp;
	margin: 0;
}
.gcd-text-col scrollbarvertical slidertrack {
	background-color: #100c06;
}
.gcd-text-col scrollbarvertical sliderbar {
	background-color: #4a4a45;
}
.gcd-text-col scrollbarvertical sliderbar:hover,
.gcd-text-col scrollbarvertical sliderbar:active {
	background-color: #6e6e66;
}
.gcd-text-col scrollbarvertical sliderarrowdec,
.gcd-text-col scrollbarvertical sliderarrowinc {
	width: 0;
	height: 0;
}
.gcd-text-col scrollbarhorizontal {
	height: 0;
}
.gcd-line {
	display: block;
	width: 100%;
	text-align: center;
	white-space: normal;
	color: #ffe0a0;
}
.gcd-line.bold { font-weight: bold; }

/* Invisible position reference for the native 3D item-preview render pass
   (GenericConfirmDialog.cpp's Render3D()); fixed reference-space size matching
   C3DItemCommonMsgBox's own MSGBOX_3DITEM_WIDTH/HEIGHT (40x40, CommonMessageBox.h) so the native
   render lands sized to match wherever this box is positioned. Renders nothing itself.

   position: static overrides base.rcss's `.layout-anchor { position: absolute; ... }` -- that
   class assumes its own explicit top/left elsewhere (main_frame.rcss's #item_hotkey_anchor, a
   fixed legacy coordinate), which this anchor doesn't have: it's meant to flow as an ordinary
   flex child of .gcd-body itself (a row, sibling of .gcd-text-col), not be pulled out of it. */
.gcd-item3d-anchor {
	position: static;
	width: 40dp;
	height: 40dp;
	flex-shrink: 0;
	margin-right: 8dp;
}
/* Same display/.hidden cascade-tie fix as .gcd-input-row/.gcd-keypad-grid below -- this anchor is
   now conditionally hidden (data-class-hidden="!has_item3d") so .gcd-text-col alone fills the row
   when there's no item3D, instead of always reserving a 40dp-wide empty slot. */
.gcd-item3d-anchor.hidden { display: none; }

/* Optional text-entry field -- see the .rml's own comment for the NumericKeypad-vs-Text split.
   `.btn.gcd-keypad-btn`/`.gcd-keypad-delete` override `.btn`'s own `position: absolute` back to
   static so they can flex-wrap into a grid instead of stacking on top of each other. */
.gcd-input-row {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	align-items: center;
	margin-top: 4dp;
}
/* .gcd-input-row sets its own `display`, so an earlier same-specificity `.hidden { display: none;
   }` (base.rcss) loses the cascade tie without this later, more specific override -- without it,
   `data-class-hidden` toggling "hidden" on this row would have no visible effect. */
.gcd-input-row.hidden { display: none; }
.gcd-keypad-display {
	color: #ffe0a0;
	font-weight: bold;
	letter-spacing: 4dp;
	min-height: 14dp;
	margin-bottom: 4dp;
}
.gcd-keypad-grid {
	display: flex;
	flex-wrap: wrap;
	justify-content: center;
	width: 150dp;
}
.gcd-keypad-grid.hidden { display: none; } /* same cascade-tie fix as .gcd-input-row above */
.btn.gcd-keypad-btn, .btn.gcd-keypad-delete {
	position: static;
	width: 26dp;
	height: 18dp;
	margin: 1dp;
	font-size: 10dp;
}
/* Position reference only, same idea as .gcd-item3d-anchor -- the native g_pSingleTextInputBox
   widget draws on top of this natively, nothing renders through RmlUi here for Mode::Text.
   position: static for the same reason .gcd-item3d-anchor needs it -- see that rule's comment. */
.gcd-input-anchor {
	position: static;
	width: 150dp;
	height: 18dp;
}

/* Optional progress bar (CProgressMsgBox/CCursedTempleProgressMsgBox) -- same
   `x_fraction * 100 + '%'` fill technique main_frame.rml's own HP/MP/AG/SD/EXP gauges prove. */
.gcd-progress-track {
	width: 150dp;
	height: 10dp;
	margin-top: 4dp;
	background-color: #100c06;
	border: 1dp #4a3620;
}
.gcd-progress-fill {
	height: 100%;
	background-color: #ffd23c;
}

/* top: 110dp = native's own vertical math for a fixed 160dp panel (160 - MSGBOX_BTN_BOTTOM_BLANK(20)
   - MSGBOX_BTN_HEIGHT(30)). Buttons stay the shared .btn sprite (op1_b_all.tga) with real bound
   text, not native's own baked OK/Cancel art -- this dialog's primary/secondary labels are
   data-driven (a future non-OK/Cancel-labeled dialog needs real text), and every current call site
   already uses the default OK/Cancel labels, so nothing is lost today either way. */
.btn.gcd-btn-primary, .btn.gcd-btn-secondary {
	top: 110dp;
	width: 54dp;
	height: 30dp;
}
.gcd-btn-primary   { left: 30dp; }
.gcd-btn-primary.solo { left: 88dp; }
.gcd-btn-secondary { left: 146dp; }
